"t":"= c4 + c2 - 1"},{"x":0.50562,"y":0.26946,"w":0.32631,"h":0.0255,"fs":0.01279,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"c2 + 5 Power or Product Rule"},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.27329,"w":0.20331,"h":0.08776,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"= 2 lim"},{"x":0.50036,"y":0.29506,"w":0.32906,"h":0.02848,"fs":0.01401,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"xS -2(4x2 - 3) Root Rule with n = 2"},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.30398,"w":0.53367,"h":0.08776,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"= 24(-2)2 - 3 Product and Multiple Rules"},{"x":0.50026,"y":0.32575,"w":0.10723,"h":0.02848,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"xS-2 4x2 - lim"},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.327,"w":0.2258,"h":0.08776,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"= 216 - 3"},{"x":0.58112,"y":0.33085,"w":0.21516,"h":0.02338,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"xS-2 3 Difference Rule"},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.35002,"w":0.19818,"h":0.08776,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"= 213"},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.43125,"w":0.59307,"h":0.07566,"fs":0.01279,"n":5,"k":1,"t":"Theorem 1 simplifies the task of calculating limits of polynomials and rational functions. To evaluate the limit of a polynomial function as x approaches c, merely substitute c for x in the formula for the function. To evaluate the limit of a rational function as x approaches a point c at which the denominator is not zero, substitute c for x in the formula for the function. (See Examples 5a and 5b.) We state these results formally as theorems."},{"x":0.35391,"y":0.54509,"w":0.26125,"h":0.03296,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"THEOREM 2—Limits of Polynomials If P(x) = an"},{"x":0.43603,"y":0.55635,"w":0.06706,"h":0.02262,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"xn + an-1"},{"x":0.50325,"y":0.55635,"w":0.15553,"h":0.0238,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"xn-1 + g + a0, then"},{"x":0.5967,"y":0.57937,"w":0.06665,"h":0.02262,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"cn + an-1"},{"x":0.66351,"y":0.57937,"w":0.12204,"h":0.0238,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"cn-1 + g + a0."},{"x":0.45068,"y":0.58385,"w":0.14587,"h":0.02305,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"lim xSc P(x) = P(c) = an"},{"x":0.3531,"y":0.65398,"w":0.35344,"h":0.03158,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"THEOREM 3—Limits of Rational Functions If P(x) and Q(x) are polynomials and Q(c) ≠0, then"},{"x":0.58171,"y":0.6917,"w":0.03327,"h":0.0236,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"P(x)"},{"x":0.58598,"y":0.6917,"w":0.08346,"h":0.03313,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Q(x) = P(c)"},{"x":0.5584,"y":0.70103,"w":0.02326,"h":0.02242,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"lim xSc"},{"x":0.64044,"y":0.70103,"w":0.03574,"h":0.02147,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Q(c)."},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.75663,"w":0.48376,"h":0.01687,"fs":0.01407,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Example 6 The following calculation illustrates Theorems 2 and 3:"},{"x":0.4985,"y":0.78274,"w":0.22988,"h":0.03993,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"x2 + 5 = (-1)3 + 4(-1)2 - 3"},{"x":0.47347,"y":0.78487,"w":0.0936,"h":0.02657,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"x3 + 4x2 - 3"},{"x":0.62577,"y":0.78997,"w":0.1353,"h":0.0327,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"(-1)2 + 5 = 0"},{"x":0.75288,"y":0.79654,"w":0.04567,"h":0.02452,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"6 = 0"},{"x":0.35276,"y":0.79717,"w":0.12068,"h":0.02338,"fs":0.01279,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"lim xS -1"},{"x":0.05994,"y":0.83518,"w":0.23554,"h":0.08698,"fs":0.01151,"n":6,"k":1,"t":"Identifying Common Factors It can be shown that if Q(x) is a poly- nomial and Q(c) = 0, then (x - c) is a factor of Q(x). Thus, if the numerator and denominator of a rational function of x are both zero at x = c, they have"},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.84893,"w":0.41174,"h":0.01658,"fs":0.01375,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Eliminating Common Factors from Zero Denominators"},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.8739,"w":0.59302,"h":0.06031,"fs":0.01279,"n":4,"k":1,"t":"Theorem 3 applies only if the denominator of the rational function is not zero at the limit point c. If the denominator is zero, canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator may reduce the fraction to one whose denominator is no longer zero at c. If this happens, we can find the limit by substitution in the simplified fraction."},{"x":0.06076,"y":0.92073,"w":0.16865,"h":0.0155,"fs":0.01151,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"(x - c) as a common factor."}]}